WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:02.252
Is it something that
 you want to direct as well?

00:00:02.293 --> 00:00:06.256
I've gone back and forth on whether
 I absolutely had to direct it or not.

00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:08.174
Let's just say you're the director. 

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:11.052
Let's just not let's not hand over
 the reins just yet.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:12.512
All right, here we go. 

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:16.016
I'm Destin Daniel Cretton, and I'm here
 chatting with Daniel Hanna.

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:17.642
Hi Daniel.

00:00:17.642 --> 00:00:19.185
Hi I’m R.J. Daniel Hanna.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:22.313
I go by Daniel and
I'm a 2021 Nicholl Fellow.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:25.817
We're talking about all
things writing and namely

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:27.485
His Nicholl winning script

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.739
Shelter Animal,
which I loved so much.

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:31.948
It’s about a female prison inmate

00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:35.493
who begins working in animal
 shelter in order to gain parole.

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:38.371
Destin Daniel Cretton is
a filmmaker I really respect 

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:40.957
and kind of inspired me
 to enter the competition.

00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:45.545
Welcome, Daniel. 

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:46.713
How are you doing?

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.673
I really loved your script,
by the way.

00:00:48.673 --> 00:00:49.758
I really loved it. 

00:00:49.758 --> 00:00:51.926
Those characters,
just like, came alive.

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:53.511
I fell in love with them. 

00:00:53.511 --> 00:00:57.432
I've always found that
 tragedy or difficult

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.478
dramatic moments are often
 kind of the best

00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:05.315
time to interject humor
 when things get really dark.

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:07.692
It's typically when
 the jokes start to fly.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:12.906
What's kind of like the stages of grief
 after you get those notes?

00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:16.034
The grief is probably when you feel like

00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:19.162
you have something that's really working
 and it's not translating.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:22.123
I have never written something in solitude

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:25.502
and found it to be ready to go and shoot.

00:01:25.543 --> 00:01:29.172
I need the reflection
 of other people's opinions

00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:31.800
to show me what's working and what's not.

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:36.137
This script I worked on for a long time
 and I did a short film and rewrote it

00:01:36.137 --> 00:01:40.517
after working with actors,
 and that process really made me realize

00:01:40.558 --> 00:01:42.268
how much extra stuff was in there 

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:45.647
in the feature that I didn't need
 and what the real heart of the story was.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:50.735
I have that tendency to
 when I'm starting to write a feature,

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:56.449
I just think like, how the hell am I going
 to fill 90 to 120 pages?

00:01:56.699 --> 00:02:01.162
And then I just start putting more and
 more characters and ideas and locations.

00:02:01.162 --> 00:02:06.376
For me, almost always,
the rewrite process is about simplifying, 

00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:08.628
simplifying, simplifying.

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.964
Something that I forget again
every single time

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.006
I start a new project.

00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:18.221
Yeah, I think in every single movie
 that I've done, there is a scene

00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:23.643
that was one of my favorite scenes
 of the movie on the editing floor.

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:28.648
The most fun scene to shoot
 the actors were on their A-game.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:32.360
Everything seemed to be working
 about the scene, except that

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.655
it wasn't necessary in the movie
 and it wasn't working in the movie.

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:39.075
I've often found that
it was a necessary scene to shoot

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:43.413
for the actors to experience
 for all of us to experience.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.874
You know, you started in
the indie space,

00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:47.959
and obviously working on
a bigger canvas now. 

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:51.462
What's it like, as an artist,
navigating that?

00:02:51.462 --> 00:02:53.673
From a process standpoint,

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:58.803
I don't see a huge difference
 between short term twelve and Marvel.

00:02:58.803 --> 00:03:02.307
Obviously, there's bigger toys,
 there's more people,

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.560
but you're still sitting on a set
 looking at a monitor

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.105
and you're seeing one shot
that you're trying to get right

00:03:09.105 --> 00:03:10.690
 and then you move
to the next one.

00:03:11.232 --> 00:03:14.527
I said no to a lot of things
 that some people would have

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:18.281
said, “You're crazy”
 at the time that I said no to them.

00:03:18.823 --> 00:03:23.203
I find that for me, being
completely myself in a pitch

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:27.582
and not trying to sell myself,
 not trying to do what I think the

00:03:27.582 --> 00:03:29.000
other people other want

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.212
may not land me the job
every time, but it does

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:34.672
weed out whether or not
I should be doing this

00:03:34.672 --> 00:03:36.007
with these people anyway.

00:03:36.257 --> 00:03:40.094
And when I look back
 at those no's, they defined me

00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:42.931
just as much as the things
 that I said yes to.

00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:45.683
Is that something
 that you want to direct as well?

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:47.727
It is. You know, I've
gone back and forth

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:50.521
 on whether I absolutely had
to direct it or not.

00:03:50.521 --> 00:03:52.357
 Let's just say you're the director.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.235
Let's just not. Let's not hand over
 the reins just yet.

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:56.611
A little premature. 

00:03:56.611 --> 00:04:00.281
Congratulations on a
 really beautiful script.

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:02.909
I enjoyed it in so many ways.

00:04:02.951 --> 00:04:06.496
Thanks so much, and thanks for reading
 and taking the time.

00:04:06.496 --> 00:04:07.497
I really appreciate it.

00:04:07.497 --> 00:04:09.374
All right Daniel. Talk soon.

00:04:09.374 --> 00:04:10.416
Talk soon.

